lwazani"},"authoredDate":"2026-08-24T13:18:39Z","committedDate":"2026-08-24T13:18:39Z","committer":{"account":null,"email":"lwazani@Laiths-MacBook-Air.local","name":"Laith Alwazani"},"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:21:47.645Z","repositoryId":1345029684,"sha":"1eb5b65c195ae065cdd5d8d07100b6abbaac50d8"},{"author":{"account":null,"email":"lwazani@Laiths-MacBook-Air.local","name":"Laith Alwazani"},"authoredDate":"2026-08-24T13:18:31Z","committedDate":"2026-08-24T13:18:31Z","committer":{"account":null,"email":"lwazani@Laiths-MacBook-Air.local","name":"Laith Alwazani"},"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:21:47.645Z","repositoryId":1345029684,"sha":"1ad0c47a1319864723192e2793c1ce0968f6772c"},{"author":{"account":null,"email":"lwazani@Laiths-MacBook-Air.local","name":"Laith Alwazani"},"authoredDate":"2026-08-24T13:18:26Z","committedDate":"2026-08-24T13:18:26Z","committer":{"account":null,"email":"lwazani@Laiths-MacBook-Air.local","name":"Laith Alwazani"},"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:21:47.645Z","repositoryId":1345029684,"sha":"8efdc465d3b99c295991d1711f73a8baa9d7e71f"},{"author":{"account":null,"email":"lwazani@laiths-macbook-air.home","name":"Laith Alwazani"},"authoredDate":"2026-08-17T11:44:32Z","committedDate":"2026-08-17T11:44:32Z","committer":{"account":null,"email":"lwazani@laiths-macbook-air.home","name":"Laith Alwazani"},"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:21:47.645Z","repositoryId":1345029684,"sha":"96c0b294e5a091e12b6b7d85689e6431c40ccdca"}],"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:21:47.645Z","raw":[{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/98265005?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wlaith/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wlaith/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wlaith/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wlaith/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wlaith","id":98265005,"login":"wlaith","node_id":"U_kgDOBdtnrQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wlaith/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wlaith/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wlaith/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wlaith/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wlaith/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wlaith","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wlaith/akhtabooti-desktop/commits/db5fff3832570758aa1c6450a50c3297ee40b8c8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-27T12:27:13Z","email":"98265005+wlaith@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Laith Alwazani"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-27T12:27:13Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Integration/public readiness (#16)\n\n* chore: add licence, security policy and real project metadata\n\nThe repository is public but carried no licence, no security policy and\nscaffold-generated identity throughout.\n\n- Add Apache-2.0 LICENSE, matching Apache Tika in the extraction path\n- Add SECURITY.md with a private reporting channel and disclosure terms\n- Fill in description, licence, repository and author in package.json and\n  Cargo.toml, which still read authors = [\"you\"]\n- Pin the supported Node version via engines\n- Replace the Vite template title and favicon in index.html\n- Remove unused scaffold assets (vite.svg, tauri.svg, vue.svg)\n- Replace the \"Placeholder ...\" copy shipping in the About dialog and the\n  guide with real text, and read the version from the app rather than\n  hardcoding it\n- Rewrite README around what the app does and the offline guarantee\n\n* chore: add contribution scaffolding and dependency updates\n\nThe repository had no contribution guidance and no structured way to file an\nissue, on a project where a careless bug report can attach someone''s real\npersonal data.\n\n- CONTRIBUTING.md covering setup, branching and the expectation that no real\n  personal data appears in issues, fixtures or screenshots\n- Issue forms for bugs and features, with the personal-data warning at the top\n  of the bug form and security reports routed to a private advisory instead\n- Pull request template prompting for what changed, how it was tested, and\n  whether the change affects what the app reads, writes or sends\n- CHANGELOG.md in Keep a Changelog format\n- Dependabot for npm, cargo and github-actions\n\nCODE_OF_CONDUCT.md is deliberately not included: it needs an enforcement\ncontact address that the project owns, which is a decision for the maintainers.\n\n* fix(ci): run CI on the branches we actually use, and cut releases from tags\n\nCI declared push/pull_request on main only, while every pull request but one\nhas targeted development, so six of seven merged without any checks. The\nrelease workflow triggered on a `release` branch that no longer exists.\n\n- Run CI on both main and development until the trunk question is settled\n- Trigger releases from `v*` tags instead of a deleted branch, so a published\n  build always corresponds to a commit reachable from a branch\n- Name releases `Akhtabooti <tag>` rather than the scaffold''s `App v__VERSION__`,\n  which collided with the hand-cut v0.1.0 tag\n- Drop the CORE_REPO_TOKEN URL rewrite: akhtabooti-core is public, and secrets\n  are unavailable to fork pull requests, so it expanded to an empty credential\n  for every github.com URL and broke outside contributors\n- Pin Node to 20 in both workflows; release was tracking `latest`\n- Publish SHA256SUMS and build provenance attestation for every release\n- Build with --locked in CI and release. Cargo.lock is committed and this is an\n  application, so the lock already decides which akhtabooti-core revision is\n  built; --locked makes that binding rather than advisory, and fails the build\n  if any dependency would move rather than moving it silently.\n- Declare akhtabooti-core once in [workspace.dependencies]. It has to appear\n  twice, as a build dependency and a normal one, and if those two ever drifted\n  Cargo would resolve two package ids and build two GraalVM copies.\n- Drop the duplicated version from tauri.conf.json and inherit it from\n  Cargo.toml\n\n* fix(security): enable CSP and remove the arbitrary file write command\n\nThe webview ran with no content security policy, and `write_file` accepted any\npath from the frontend and wrote any content to it. Together those meant a\ncontent injection bug would have been a write to any file the user could write.\n\n- Set a restrictive CSP. The app loads no remote resources, so this costs\n  nothing: everything is ''self'' plus the local IPC channel.\n- Replace `write_file` with `export_report`, which opens the save dialog on the\n  Rust side. The destination is never supplied by the webview, so there is no\n  longer a command that writes to a caller-chosen path.\n- Document what the app reads, writes, sends and retains, and what it does not\n  protect against, in docs/threat-model.md\n\nVerified the app starts and runs with the CSP applied.\n\n* fix(macos): drop ad-hoc signing and make the verifier catch Gatekeeper rejection\n\nThe published v0.1.0 macOS build is ad-hoc signed, unnotarised, and rejected by\nGatekeeper on any machine. verify-macos-bundle.sh passed it anyway: every check\nit ran was about whether dyld could load the binary, and none asked whether\nmacOS would let a user open it.\n\n- Remove \"signingIdentity\": \"-\" from tauri.conf.json. Ad-hoc signatures have no\n  Team ID, cannot be notarised, and the key also competed with the\n  APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY that CI provides.\n- Add check 6, requiring a stapled notarisation ticket.\n- Add check 7, requiring spctl to report the bundle as accepted.\n\nVerified against the published v0.1.0 artifact: checks 1-5 still pass, 6 and 7\nfail, and the script exits 1 instead of reporting success.\n\nThe APPLE_CERTIFICATE secret still needs replacing; that is not in this change.\n\n* chore: add linting and formatting tooling\n\nThe project had no ESLint, Prettier, editorconfig or hooks, so style\nconsistency depended entirely on who was typing.\n\n- ESLint 9 flat config with the TypeScript and Vue recommended sets, with\n  eslint-config-prettier last so the two do not fight\n- Prettier with a 100 column width, matching the existing double-quote and\n  semicolon style\n- .editorconfig so editors agree before anything runs\n- lint, lint:fix, format and format:check scripts\n\nNo source files are reformatted in this commit; that follows separately so this\none stays reviewable. Currently reports zero errors and three warnings.\n\n* style: apply Prettier formatting\n\nMechanical only — no behaviour change. Produced by `npm run format` with the\nconfiguration added in the previous commit.\n\nVerified afterwards: build and typecheck pass, ESLint reports zero errors.\n\n* Update README to remove macOS signing note","tree":{"sha":"a646830e7531cdad183ea0aec21d7cba3e0971b0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wlaith/akhtabooti-desktop/git/trees/a646830e7531cdad183ea0aec21d7cba3e0971b0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wlaith/akhtabooti-desktop/git/commits/db5fff3832570758aa1c6450a50c3297ee40b8c8","verification":{"payload":"tree a646830e7531cdad183ea0aec21d7cba3e0971b0\nparent d8a8166752c1637c78ee3cbe857a83f5884fef00\nauthor Laith Alwazani <98265005+wlaith@users.noreply.github.com> 1790512033 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790512033 +0300\n\nIntegration/public readiness (#16)\n\n* chore: add licence, security policy and real project metadata\n\nThe repository is public but carried no licence, no security policy and\nscaffold-generated identity throughout.\n\n- Add Apache-2.0 LICENSE, matching Apache Tika in the extraction path\n- Add SECURITY.md with a private reporting channel and disclosure terms\n- Fill in description, licence, repository and author in package.json and\n  Cargo.toml, which still read authors = [\"you\"]\n- Pin the supported Node version via engines\n- Replace the Vite template title and favicon in index.html\n- Remove unused scaffold assets (vite.svg, tauri.svg, vue.svg)\n- Replace the \"Placeholder ...\" copy shipping in the About dialog and the\n  guide with real text, and read the version from the app rather than\n  hardcoding it\n- Rewrite README around what the app does and the offline guarantee\n\n* chore: add contribution scaffolding and dependency updates\n\nThe repository had no contribution guidance and no structured way to file an\nissue, on a project where a careless bug report can attach someone''s real\npersonal data.\n\n- CONTRIBUTING.md covering setup, branching and the expectation that no real\n  personal data appears in issues, fixtures or screenshots\n- Issue forms for bugs and features, with the personal-data warning at the top\n  of the bug form and security reports routed to a private advisory instead\n- Pull request template prompting for what changed, how it was tested, and\n  whether the change affects what the app reads, writes or sends\n- CHANGELOG.md in Keep a Changelog format\n- Dependabot for npm, cargo and github-actions\n\nCODE_OF_CONDUCT.md is deliberately not included: it needs an enforcement\ncontact address that the project owns, which is a decision for the maintainers.\n\n* fix(ci): run CI on the branches we actually use, and cut releases from tags\n\nCI declared push/pull_request on main only, while every pull request but one\nhas targeted development, so six of seven merged without any checks. The\nrelease workflow triggered on a `release` branch that no longer exists.\n\n- Run CI on both main and development until the trunk question is settled\n- Trigger releases from `v*` tags instead of a deleted branch, so a published\n  build always corresponds to a commit reachable from a branch\n- Name releases `Akhtabooti <tag>` rather than the scaffold''s `App v__VERSION__`,\n  which collided with the hand-cut v0.1.0 tag\n- Drop the CORE_REPO_TOKEN URL rewrite: akhtabooti-core is public, and secrets\n  are unavailable to fork pull requests, so it expanded to an empty credential\n  for every github.com URL and broke outside contributors\n- Pin Node to 20 in both workflows; release was tracking `latest`\n- Publish SHA256SUMS and build provenance attestation for every release\n- Build with --locked in CI and release. Cargo.lock is committed and this is an\n  application, so the lock already decides which akhtabooti-core revision is\n  built; --locked makes that binding rather than advisory, and fails the build\n  if any dependency would move rather than moving it silently.\n- Declare akhtabooti-core once in [workspace.dependencies]. It has to appear\n  twice, as a build dependency and a normal one, and if those two ever drifted\n  Cargo would resolve two package ids and build two GraalVM copies.\n- Drop the duplicated version from tauri.conf.json and inherit it from\n  Cargo.toml\n\n* fix(security): enable CSP and remove the arbitrary file write command\n\nThe webview ran with no content security policy, and `write_file` accepted any\npath from the frontend and wrote any content to it. Together those meant a\ncontent injection bug would have been a write to any file the user could write.\n\n- Set a restrictive CSP. The app loads no remote resources, so this costs\n  nothing: everything is ''self'' plus the local IPC channel.\n- Replace `write_file` with `export_report`, which opens the save dialog on the\n  Rust side. The destination is never supplied by the webview, so there is no\n  longer a command that writes to a caller-chosen path.\n- Document what the app reads, writes, sends and retains, and what it does not\n  protect against, in docs/threat-model.md\n\nVerified the app starts and runs with the CSP applied.\n\n* fix(macos): drop ad-hoc signing and make the verifier catch Gatekeeper rejection\n\nThe published v0.1.0 macOS build is ad-hoc signed, unnotarised, and rejected by\nGatekeeper on any machine. verify-macos-bundle.sh passed it anyway: every check\nit ran was about whether dyld could load the binary, and none asked whether\nmacOS would let a user open it.\n\n- Remove \"signingIdentity\": \"-\" from tauri.conf.json. Ad-hoc signatures have no\n  Team ID, cannot be notarised, and the key also competed with the\n  APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY that CI provides.\n- Add check 6, requiring a stapled notarisation ticket.\n- Add check 7, requiring spctl to report the bundle as accepted.\n\nVerified against the published v0.1.0 artifact: checks 1-5 still pass, 6 and 7\nfail, and the script exits 1 instead of reporting success.\n\nThe APPLE_CERTIFICATE secret still needs replacing; that is not in this change.\n\n* chore: add linting and formatting tooling\n\nThe project had no ESLint, Prettier, editorconfig or hooks, so style\nconsistency depended entirely on who was typing.\n\n- ESLint 9 flat config with the TypeScript and Vue recommended sets, with\n  eslint-config-prettier last so the two do not fight\n- Prettier with a 100 column width, matching the existing double-quote and\n  semicolon style\n- .editorconfig so editors agree before anything runs\n- lint, lint:fix, format and format:check scripts\n\nNo source files are reformatted in this commit; that follows separately so this\none stays reviewable. Currently reports zero errors and three warnings.\n\n* style: apply Prettier formatting\n\nMechanical only — no behaviour change. Produced by `npm run format` with the\nconfiguration added in the previous commit.\n\nVerified afterwards: build and typecheck pass, ESLint reports zero errors.\n\n* Update README to remove macOS signing note","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJquQuhCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAliEQADRhSekTUKRIpv5ddp7C+Maf\n8IK3jN0+L7ZRUWhq6nxMNFY5tYUmbbM/VsetHVGB6OcfLXJpl6A6uYbhX4DtoGO1\nQwvjgxXMktxKvX5wobWmH5ZLDOIQHHW36V1YIZgsc4AUH4eJYv2DsKilBenhWVyw\nDVONI07Da7QVxU2CqkKt2fq7IDSOUl9ehWVIFuG5+VWIl4v74EEDRrlKV5FgmUCi\nPezF3v1JqCKx8rMpDgR