20281a93b20b713b2dc9a648"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4073304?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/claudioed","id":4073304,"login":"claudioed","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQwNzMzMDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/order-management/commits/8a1f27b28b0e58172aef3a2ea74eb588cb052420/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-28T00:55:44Z","email":"claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com","name":"Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-28T00:55:44Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(scalability): per-workload HPA + pgxpool MaxConns/statement_timeout tuning (ADR 0026) (#110)\n\nPhase 3 (scalability) of the fleet production-readiness plan, built on\nPhase 0 (boot-retry), Phase 1 (idempotency), and Phase 2 (resilience,\nADR-0025). Phase 3.1 (chart-selector-check fix) was already done and\nverified fleet-wide before this PR.\n\nHorizontalPodAutoscaler (autoscaling/v2), one per independently\nassessed Deployment:\n\n- api: min 1 / max 4 / target 70% CPU. Stateless OLTP HTTP; its\n  per-process-unique-group Kafka caches and RepromiseConsumer''s stable\n  shared group are both safe at N replicas.\n- analytics-projector: min 1 / max 2 / target 70% CPU (capped below\n  api''s 4). Its AnalyticsConsumerGroup is a stable shared Kafka group\n  (verified against the OrderId-keyed publisher partitioning), so N\n  replicas share partitions safely, but wider fan-out buys little for\n  a lightweight idempotent-upsert workload.\n- analytics-reports: min 1 / max 3 / target 70% CPU. Stateless\n  read-only REST reader, same treatment as api.\n- frontend: min 1 / max 3 / target 70% CPU. Pure static-asset serving,\n  no server-side state.\n- mcp: deliberately NO autoscaling block at all (not just disabled).\n  The MCP Go SDK''s StreamableHTTPHandler keeps in-memory session state\n  keyed by Mcp-Session-Id with no sticky routing configured on\n  mcp-service.yaml; >1 replica can misroute a mid-session request.\n  Documented in values.yaml and the ADR; revisit if/when an external\n  session store is adopted.\n\nEvery autoscaling.<component>.enabled defaults to false in values.yaml\nso this PR changes no production replica behavior on merge. Each\nDeployment template guards its replicas: field with\n`{{- if not .Values.autoscaling.<x>.enabled }}` so a hardcoded replica\ncount never fights an active HPA controller — verified with\nhelm template across every enabled/disabled combination (0 HPAs by\ndefault, 4 HPAs with everything on, mixed enablement only removes\nreplicas: from the matching Deployment).\n\npgxpool tuning: explicit MaxConns replacing the CPU-derived library\ndefault (max(4, NumCPU())):\n\n- OLTP pool (postgres.NewPool, cmd/order + cmd/mcp): MaxConns=10.\n  Sized against the shared Postgres instance''s real max_connections=100\n  (confirmed live via `SHOW max_connections`, an unmodified Bitnami\n  default — this service shares ONE Postgres instance with up to 9\n  fleet siblings, not a dedicated one). At api''s proposed HPA ceiling\n  of 4 replicas, 4*10=40, the plan''s ~40% budget for this one service''s\n  OLTP path alone.\n- Analytics writer pool (analyticsstore.NewPool, cmd/order-projector):\n  MaxConns=5. Fixed at 1 replica, single-row upserts.\n- Analytics reader pool (analyticsstore.NewReadOnlyPool,\n  cmd/order-reports): MaxConns=5. At reports'' HPA ceiling of 3,\n  3*5=15.\n\nstatement_timeout via pgxpool.Config.AfterConnect (SET on every new\nconnection, not per-query):\n\n- OLTP: 5s — all queries are single-aggregate lookups by id, normally\n  low-single-digit ms.\n- Analytics writer: 10s — backlog-replay upserts in a tight loop, no\n  replica to fail over to.\n- Analytics reader: 15s — caller-chosen date-range aggregations, wider\n  than OLTP''s shape but still bounded.\n\nNew internal/adapters/outbound/postgres/pool_limits_integration_test.go\nproves both settings against a real testcontainers Postgres (not a\nmock): statement_timeout is read back via SHOW and a real pg_sleep is\nconfirmed cancelled by Postgres at the configured timeout (SQLSTATE\n57014); MaxConns is proven to actually block a further Acquire once\nexhausted.\n\nADR 0026 documents both halves: the per-workload HPA table with each\nDeployment''s specific safety reasoning, why mcp is excluded outright,\nthe shared-vs-dedicated-Postgres finding, and the connection-budget\naccounting including the honest worst-case (80/100 connections for\nthis service alone if every proposed HPA maxes out simultaneously)\nflagged as a documented residual risk for fleet-wide follow-up, not a\nsolved one. A read replica for analytics/reports (plan 3.4''s last\nbullet) remains explicitly out of scope.\n\nVerified: go build/vet (plain + integration), gofmt clean, make check,\nmake arch-test, go test -tags=integration ./... -race -count=1 (one\npre-existing unrelated flaky race in\nTestRepromiseConsumer_PoisonMessage_GoesToDeadLetterTopicWithoutBlockingPartition,\nconfirmed byte-identical to origin/develop and failing there too — not\nintroduced by this change), make coverage (97.9%, gate 90%), helm\nlint, helm template smoke render (default + all-enabled + mixed).","tree":{"sha":"321f269d23fbfc5dedacc1d319c3677157d280e4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/order-management/git/trees/321f269d23fbfc5dedacc1d319c3677157d280e4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/order-management/git/commits/8a1f27b28b0e58172aef3a2ea74eb588cb052420","verification":{"payload":"tree 321f269d23fbfc5dedacc1d319c3677157d280e4\nparent d65072f0914076703d11e333e5865bead84c4389\nauthor Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira <claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com> 1790556944 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790556944 -0300\n\nfeat(scalability): per-workload HPA + pgxpool MaxConns/statement_timeout tuning (ADR 0026) (#110)\n\nPhase 3 (scalability) of the fleet production-readiness plan, built on\nPhase 0 (boot-retry), Phase 1 (idempotency), and Phase 2 (resilience,\nADR-0025). Phase 3.1 (chart-selector-check fix) was already done and\nverified fleet-wide before this PR.\n\nHorizontalPodAutoscaler (autoscaling/v2), one per independently\nassessed Deployment:\n\n- api: min 1 / max 4 / target 70% CPU. Stateless OLTP HTTP; its\n  per-process-unique-group Kafka caches and RepromiseConsumer''s stable\n  shared group are both safe at N replicas.\n- analytics-projector: min 1 / max 2 / target 70% CPU (capped below\n  api''s 4). Its AnalyticsConsumerGroup is a stable shared Kafka group\n  (verified against the OrderId-keyed publisher partitioning), so N\n  replicas share partitions safely, but wider fan-out buys little for\n  a lightweight idempotent-upsert workload.\n- analytics-reports: min 1 / max 3 / target 70% CPU. Stateless\n  read-only REST reader, same treatment as api.\n- frontend: min 1 / max 3 / target 70% CPU. Pure static-asset serving,\n  no server-side state.\n- mcp: deliberately NO autoscaling block at all (not just disabled).\n  The MCP Go SDK''s StreamableHTTPHandler keeps in-memory session state\n  keyed by Mcp-Session-Id with no sticky routing configured on\n  mcp-service.yaml; >1 replica can misroute a mid-session request.\n  Documented in values.yaml and the ADR; revisit if/when an external\n  session store is adopted.\n\nEvery autoscaling.<component>.enabled defaults to false in values.yaml\nso this PR changes no production replica behavior on merge. Each\nDeployment template guards its replicas: field with\n`{{- if not .Values.autoscaling.<x>.enabled }}` so a hardcoded replica\ncount never fights an active HPA controller — verified with\nhelm template across every enabled/disabled combination (0 HPAs by\ndefault, 4 HPAs with everything on, mixed enablement only removes\nreplicas: from the matching Deployment).\n\npgxpool tuning: explicit MaxConns replacing the CPU-derived library\ndefault (max(4, NumCPU())):\n\n- OLTP pool (postgres.NewPool, cmd/order + cmd/mcp): MaxConns=10.\n  Sized against the shared Postgres instance''s real max_connections=100\n  (confirmed live via `SHOW max_connections`, an unmodified Bitnami\n  default — this service shares ONE Postgres instance with up to 9\n  fleet siblings, not a dedicated one). At api''s proposed HPA ceiling\n  of 4 replicas, 4*10=40, the plan''s ~40% budget for this one service''s\n  OLTP path alone.\n- Analytics writer pool (analyticsstore.NewPool, cmd/order-projector):\n  MaxConns=5. Fixed at 1 replica, single-row upserts.\n- Analytics reader pool (analyticsstore.NewReadOnlyPool,\n  cmd/order-reports): MaxConns=5. At reports'' HPA ceiling of 3,\n  3*5=15.\n\nstatement_timeout via pgxpool.Config.AfterConnect (SET on every new\nconnection, not per-query):\n\n- OLTP: 5s — all queries are single-aggregate lookups by id, normally\n  low-single-digit ms.\n- Analytics writer: 10s — backlog-replay upserts in a tight loop, no\n  replica to fail over to.\n- Analytics reader: 15s — caller-chosen date-range aggregations, wider\n  than OLTP''s shape but still bounded.\n\nNew internal/adapters/outbound/postgres/pool_limits_integration_test.go\nproves both settings against a real testcontainers Postgres (not a\nmock): statement_timeout is read back via SHOW and a real pg_sleep is\nconfirmed cancelled by Postgres at the configured timeout (SQLSTATE\n57014); MaxConns is proven to actually block a further Acquire once\nexhausted.\n\nADR 0026 documents both halves: the per-workload HPA table with each\nDeployment''s specific safety reasoning, why mcp is excluded outright,\nthe shared-vs-dedicated-Postgres finding, and the connection-budget\naccounting including the honest worst-case (80/100 connections for\nthis service alone if every proposed HPA maxes out simultaneously)\nflagged as a documented residual risk for fleet-wide follow-up, not a\nsolved one. A read replica for analytics/reports (plan 3.4''s last\nbullet) remains explicitly out of scope.\n\nVerified: go build/vet (plain + integration), gofmt clean, make check,\nmake arch-test, go test -tags=integration ./... -race -count=1 (one\npre-existing unrelated flaky race in\nTestRepromiseConsumer_PoisonMessage_GoesToDeadLetterTopicWithoutBlockingPartition,\nconfirmed byte-identical to origin/develop and failing there too — not\nintroduced by this change), make coverage (97.9%, gate 90%), helm\nlint, helm template smoke render (default + all-enabled + mixed).","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqubsQCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAoawQADToYyByyAhTZTs6XF/4xO51\npD1xLpBpVUEFSi3FTQwIb6g5lbgGF29i4I4TEINWS13/aWhOmB6+d2V+B9mqPwDi\n7KxfhBfwVrI8ysNj1G3PaQDXTh/whxL3lOku0v+maRNHC7yuuGOi7JUv21JE32rg\n9ZELt4zpZoCEDdJZp3tjDFIuW0vsWbULX8N1luDkj1wrhK+gmOUWr6zNUNrBya9b\neERhm+zURVzi8S/b1laJW0UMcr0Oi2Lp10B1RMZOEZlFjplbQLmtmsrSLdBJxxZ/\npekzXAu6d+oBpxQu2gNenyQ+19aM2hL/5TDCcPryRYcwSGual72IGARQEq8MPnTt\n68amLKkML4A7SCI8MnQx8vGk8kJ5eRIxKj/ad43PiiU0+qI6TAXLraCuCwvI4QdY\n0lDjb+m/hh7kMkCFDALy7YrdrYIEY0/QNylUan9962XYvtvJYECz4QKScXDz/Z/E\nlgY1KIFirrgMvO+lD/F9nVT2AMeSaAmMZsvDHmuUZeuI+QXUzo8g4aUixFkzWSjK\nxiYo8x58ZvIoCKtEYVEg4KH8C7kG5BGQ3z2YK397JshdGp8oltLZWCubbuDjMiot\nF5xNujmJ5Zf+QkX6cE+6RQnIEyLvijoT1VEQDi22XVF1xbN48ZOSlQmd01PIZNbo\n7Jytw+8SxLHCJj3yg+VF\n=cEb9\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":tru