rl":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/claudioed","id":4073304,"login":"claudioed","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQwNzMzMDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/warehouse-infra/commits/e9f17306c0c82725938d6e1b947d679c7494c677/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-05T17:18:52Z","email":"claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com","name":"Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-05T17:18:52Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(kafka): serve host clients from the in-cluster broker, retire compose Kafka (#6)\n\nThe fleet was running TWO Kafkas: the Terraform-managed in-cluster release\n(kafka.tf) that every pod uses, and a separate docker-compose broker\n(container \"warehouse-kafka\", ~/warehouse-systems/docker-compose.kafka.yml)\non host port 9092 that the out-of-cluster consumers used -- the e2e-tests\nharness (env.sh pins KAFKA_BROKERS=localhost:9092) and local `go run`\ndevelopment.\n\nTwo brokers sharing a name and a port number but no data is a topology, not\na convenience: an event published in-cluster was invisible to a host\nconsumer and vice versa, so which broker you happened to be pointed at\nsilently changed observed behaviour. The compose broker was also outside\nTerraform entirely, so `terraform destroy` never touched it and it drifted\n(it had been up 41 hours across several cluster rebuilds).\n\nGive the single in-cluster broker a second advertised listener instead.\nKafka clients bootstrap by asking the broker for its address and then\nreconnect to whatever it answers, so a broker serving both audiences must\nadvertise a different address to each: in-cluster pods keep the pod''s\nheadless DNS name on CLIENT, host clients get EXTERNAL://localhost:9092.\nThat is why this uses the chart''s externalAccess rather than another plain\nNodePort Service like exposure.tf''s UIs -- a plain Service would route the\nTCP connection correctly and then hand the host client an unreachable\nin-cluster address on the very next round trip.\n\nTaking NodePort 9092 (matching the host port, so there is one Kafka port\nfleet-wide) requires widening the apiserver''s --service-node-port-range,\nwhich the default 30000-32767 forbids; done via a kubeadm config patch on\nthe control-plane node. Kong and the observability UIs stay in the 30000s.\n\nVerified on a full destroy/apply rebuild: advertised.listeners resolves to\nCLIENT://...svc.cluster.local:9092 + EXTERNAL://localhost:9092; a host-side\nproduce/consume round trip on localhost:9092 succeeds and the resulting\ntopic is visible in-cluster alongside the live warehouse.* topics; all\n7 services'' consumer groups are connected; 42/42 pods healthy; smoke test\npasses; terraform plan reports no changes.\n\nNote: ~/warehouse-systems/docker-compose.kafka.yml is not tracked in any\nrepo (that directory is not a git repo) and was removed locally; each\nservice repo''s own docker-compose.kafka.yml is untouched and still serves\nstandalone single-service development.","tree":{"sha":"c935779cda2b9e58c0dad2886c54a3b8db55c20a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/warehouse-infra/git/trees/c935779cda2b9e58c0dad2886c54a3b8db55c20a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/warehouse-infra/git/commits/e9f17306c0c82725938d6e1b947d679c7494c677","verification":{"payload":"tree c935779cda2b9e58c0dad2886c54a3b8db55c20a\nparent 4b6d5d715323d7e1fb12fb5e3bb241963a36007a\nauthor Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira <claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com> 1788628732 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788628732 -0300\n\nfeat(kafka): serve host clients from the in-cluster broker, retire compose Kafka (#6)\n\nThe fleet was running TWO Kafkas: the Terraform-managed in-cluster release\n(kafka.tf) that every pod uses, and a separate docker-compose broker\n(container \"warehouse-kafka\", ~/warehouse-systems/docker-compose.kafka.yml)\non host port 9092 that the out-of-cluster consumers used -- the e2e-tests\nharness (env.sh pins KAFKA_BROKERS=localhost:9092) and local `go run`\ndevelopment.\n\nTwo brokers sharing a name and a port number but no data is a topology, not\na convenience: an event published in-cluster was invisible to a host\nconsumer and vice versa, so which broker you happened to be pointed at\nsilently changed observed behaviour. The compose broker was also outside\nTerraform entirely, so `terraform destroy` never touched it and it drifted\n(it had been up 41 hours across several cluster rebuilds).\n\nGive the single in-cluster broker a second advertised listener instead.\nKafka clients bootstrap by asking the broker for its address and then\nreconnect to whatever it answers, so a broker serving both audiences must\nadvertise a different address to each: in-cluster pods keep the pod''s\nheadless DNS name on CLIENT, host clients get EXTERNAL://localhost:9092.\nThat is why this uses the chart''s externalAccess rather than another plain\nNodePort Service like exposure.tf''s UIs -- a plain Service would route the\nTCP connection correctly and then hand the host client an unreachable\nin-cluster address on the very next round trip.\n\nTaking NodePort 9092 (matching the host port, so there is one Kafka port\nfleet-wide) requires widening the apiserver''s --service-node-port-range,\nwhich the default 30000-32767 forbids; done via a kubeadm config patch on\nthe control-plane node. Kong and the observability UIs stay in the 30000s.\n\nVerified on a full destroy/apply rebuild: advertised.listeners resolves to\nCLIENT://...svc.cluster.local:9092 + EXTERNAL://localhost:9092; a host-side\nproduce/consume round trip on localhost:9092 succeeds and the resulting\ntopic is visible in-cluster alongside the live warehouse.* topics; all\n7 services'' consumer groups are connected; 42/42 pods healthy; smoke test\npasses; terraform plan reports no changes.\n\nNote: ~/warehouse-systems/docker-compose.kafka.yml is not tracked in any\nrepo (that directory is not a git repo) and was removed locally; each\nservice repo''s own docker-compose.kafka.yml is untouched and still serves\nstandalone single-service development.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnE78CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAOmIQABdx4AsKwhu7SCO/aoTHd1fz\nfsLQ4FqZK43lGdXau0d8fz/ybthvpeHCEIZ1me1BAVj+S+QG8ybLi4Rv1NdiOhes\nc/kGtyB591ZnklvsFj/kR28PRm3ryXLSDmZ04IJzZQGFO0bgNPx6WzewLOuGrn5e\nbXL/h63O9PcMlBK37uW3ZLe5RKZbuoED109Xw1QFMA/Hn0PvlTUsMf1Qlb2dT6Al\nNyM5BGzu1SXviU7DAE+JHOT8JI28nA8eWWhqFye5LLc8jOtlLckU3pMjG6WqeMhh\n2Bgdg591ScZuVE+fpINPzNQrPVwJhb6b+T5lEcgdYjwP+bG/B1ZdfOdEjqJFfYA6\nGFb2MBbCm9rj9dy9/Kwo8gLQJiZBDnn5NFHzpSdXw+4LG4QejPmVi0V/OMO2bnh0\n1QDGu1/kvjELNpSW0eJPvfDr47BMQNBU2U6MxT30Knsz8P3tukFtv1HGXihW9xw0\nbpTb159Uxd70049QYB3GoqEpyA2pcHU4RQSlWaQNIspKZVfU4PDyCgmjn0SJgyyh\n2PbxSGXfsasvLFCuktGZbirTxdEDUAlr/rfR0NZrstvKyVkuh5JfyyZdypLUmW12\nH1frYwZ/P8l/nMobwru6+sztu109KoleVmzJfFrueMi6kgxiDflCYiD/1JmQUGYz\nsfEO9uJmAJR4G5+BL2Vk\n=Edw3\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-05T17:18:52Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/warehouse-infra/commit/e9f17306c0c82725938d6e1b947d679c7494c677","node_id":"C_kwDOUEmPD9oAKGU5ZjE3MzA2YzBjODI3MjU5MzhkNmUxYjk0N2Q2NzljNzQ5NGM2Nzc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/warehouse-infra/commit/4b6d5d715323d7e1fb12fb5e3bb241963a36007a","sha":"4b6d5d715323d7e1fb12fb5e3bb241963a36007a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/warehouse-infra/commits/4b6d5d715323d7e1fb12fb5e3bb241963a36007a"}],"sha":"e9f17306c0c82725938d6e1b947d679c7494c677","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/warehouse-infra/commits/e9f17306c0c82725938d6e1b947d679c7494c677"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4073304?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/claudioed","id":4073304,"login":"claudioed","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQwNzMzMDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/warehouse-infra/commits/4b6d5d715323d7e1fb12fb5e3bb241963a36007a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-05T15:05:41Z","email":"claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com","name":"Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-05T15:05:41Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: expose observability + add Kiali, wire process-path catalogue (#5)\n\nLocalhost exposure (no more kubectl port-forward):\n- Grafana, Jaeger, and Prometheus each get a dedicated Terraform-owned\n  NodePort Service (exposure.tf) selecting the same pod labels their own\n  chart''s Service already uses -- avoids depending on each chart''s\n  differing/absent nodePort value schemas (Jaeger''s chart has no settable\n  nodePort at all).\n- kind''s extraPortMappings (main.tf) publish those NodePorts on fixed host\n  ports, same pattern Kong''s proxy already used: Grafana on :3000, Jaeger\n  on :16686, Prometheus on :9090 -- reachable the instant terraform apply\n  finishes, surviving pod restarts and laptop sleep unlike a port-forward\n  process.\n- outputs.tf: observability_port_forwards -> observability_urls (fixed\n  http://localhost:<port> URLs instead of kubectl commands); scripts/up.sh\n  updated to print them.\n\nKiali (new):\n- kiali.tf: kiali/kiali-server 2.31.0 installed into istio-system,\n  wired to the cluster''s existing Prometheus/Jaeger/Grafana (no second\n  metrics backend). auth.strategy=anonymous -- no IdP in this cluster,\n  matching the fleet''s existing no-OAuth MCP auth decision, and Kiali is\n  only ever reachable from localhost via its own NodePort. LOCAL DEV ONLY.\n- Exposed at localhost:20001 via the same NodePort+extraPortMappings\n  pattern as the observability UIs above.\n- Gated on both var.deploy_kiali and var.deploy_observability (Kiali is\n  not useful without the Prometheus it reads from).\n\nProcess-path catalogue wiring (locals.tf, services.tf):\n- fulfillment-execution, wes-work-planning, and workforce-management all\n  fail fast at boot without PATH_CATALOGUE_FILE -- a real deploy gap found\n  provisioning a fresh cluster (their charts never templated a ConfigMap\n  for it; fixed in each service''s own repo in a companion PR). This repo''s\n  config/process-paths/sortable-fc.yaml is the fleet''s single published-\n  language source of truth for it -- now read once here and fed into all\n  three charts'' new pathCatalogue.content value so they can never disagree.\n\nVerified: terraform fmt clean, terraform validate passes. Kiali''s exact\nvalues schema (deployment.resources, not top-level resources) confirmed\nagainst the real chart via helm template dry-runs before committing.","tree":{"sha":"9b5d57a5abe2556dac04c3ecd082920f46604100","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/warehouse-infra/git/trees/9b5d57a5abe2556dac04c3ecd082920f46604100"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/warehouse-infra/git/commits/4b6d5d715323d7e1fb12fb5e3bb241963a36007a","verification":{"payload":"tree 9b5d57a5abe2556dac04c3ecd082920f46604100\nparent a30dc419524bef52ba4ffbd1121e0d86b266cac4\nauthor Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira <claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com> 1788620741 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788620741 -0300\n\nfeat: expose observability + add Kiali, wire process-path catalogue (#5)\n\nLocalhost exposure (no more kubectl port-forward):\n- Grafana, Jaeger, and Prometheus each get a dedicated Terraform-owned\n  NodePort Service (exposure.tf) selecting the same pod labels their own\n  chart''s Service already uses -- avoids depending on each chart''s\n  differing/absent nodePort value schemas (Jaeger''s chart has no settable\n  nodePort at all).\n- kind''s extraPortMappings (main.tf) publish those NodePorts on fixed host\n  ports, same pattern Kong''s proxy already used: Grafana on :3000, Jaeger\n  on :16686, Prometheus on :9090 -- reachable the instant terraform apply\n  finishes, surviving pod restarts and laptop sleep unlike a port-forward\n  process.\n- outputs.tf: observability_port_forwards -> observability_urls (fixed\n  http://localhost:<port> URLs instead of kubectl commands); scripts/up.sh\n  updated to print them.\n\nKiali (new):\n- kiali.tf: kiali/kiali-server 2.31.0 installed into istio-system,\n  wired to the cluster''s existing Prometheus/Jaeger/Grafana (no second\n  metrics backend). auth.strategy=anonymous -- no IdP in this cluster,\n  matching the fleet''s existing no-OAuth MCP auth decision, and Kiali is\n  only ever reachable from localhost via its own NodePort. LOCAL DEV ONLY.\n- Exposed at localhost:20001 via the same NodePort+extraPortMappings\n  pattern as the observability UIs above.\n- Gated on both var.deploy_kiali and var.deploy_observability (Kiali is\n  not useful without the Prometheus it reads from).\n\nProcess-path catalogue wiring (locals.tf, services.tf):\n- fulfillment-execution, wes-work-planning, and workforce-management all\n  fail fast at boot without PATH_CATALOGUE_FILE -- a real deploy gap found\n  provisioning a fresh cluster (their charts never templated a ConfigMap\n  for it; fixed in each service''s own repo in a companion PR). This repo''s\n  config/process-paths/sortable-fc.yaml is the fleet''s single published-\n  language source of truth for it -- now read once here and fed into all\n  three charts'' new pathCatalogue.content value so they can never disagree.\n\nVerified: terraform fmt clean, terraform validate passes. Kiali''s exact\nvalues schema (deployment.resources, not top-level resources) confirmed\nagainst the real chart via helm template dry-runs before committing.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnC/FCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAADgwQAI/qz1jRX4GkiNYMxdfhXNNI\nn2Nn0XLtijj2hoaXg63FUqF9pJBwugPDAzIiJ8m9LQdrhbVtts09eUxQFWTzQFPy\nq65mGPP2IN8lCfTlDWUhF3dq1AHWz5oFzka96zZGWrLZ/I7NifBA2tZfFg4AU2oR